{
 "answer": "Close provides two primary authentication methods for its REST API: API Keys and OAuth 2.0 [1][2].\n\nAPI Keys are intended for internal tools, scripts, and server-side integrations [3][1]. They use HTTP Basic authentication, where the API key serves as the username and the password is left empty [3][1]. You can generate and manage these keys in your account under Settings, Developer, API Keys [3][2].\n\n```bash\ncurl https://api.close.com/api/v1/me/ -u yourapikey:\n```\n\nOAuth 2.0 is recommended for user-facing integrations [1][2]. It uses the authorization code flow, requiring you to register an OAuth app in your Close settings to obtain a Client ID and Client Secret [4][5][2].",
